Found while closing #54.

Measured on Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55): GET /containers/{id}/stats?stream=true opened
on a container that is genuinely RUNNING does not end when that container exits. It
degrades instead. One 20 s probe against a container that exited after 3 s:

  3 real readings, then 13 zero-filled ones, connection still open at 20 s

Podman 5.4.2 ends the same stream on the container's exit (5.0 s, last object a whole
reading). So on Docker this stream has no terminator tied to the container at all.

Consequence: the distribution's standing advice for streaming endpoints -- bound the
window -- has nothing to bind here, and 'wait for the stream to end' is not a strategy
on that engine. Today the only way out is on_event plus the caller's own $stop->(),
where the caller must itself decide a zero-filled object is not a reading.

#54 is closed as deliberately not guarded: a pre-flight inspect answers 'running now'
while the hazard is 'stops at any point in the stream', which the measurement above is
exactly a case of. That reasoning does not extend to the general problem.

To decide, none of it done:
  - whether the transport should grow a read timeout at all (touches Role::HTTP, the
    seam under all twelve modules -- this is the general version of #52's hang too)
  - whether the streaming methods want a max_events / deadline option so a bound can
    be expressed without writing a counting callback by hand
  - whether stats specifically should stop on Go's zero time in read, which is
    Docker's shape and not anybody's contract

Related: #45 (Podman error inside a 200, now croaked), #52 (attach hang), #53 (attach
guard), #54 (this measurement's origin).
